Answer: The program uses an abstract Shape class and three child classes. Each child class accepts its required dimensions and overrides calculateArea(). A Shape reference is used to process all three objects.Sample Input:Square side: 5Rectangle length: 4Rectangle breadth: 6Circle radius: 3Sample Output:Square area: 25.00Rectangle area: 24.00Circle area: 28.26

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
abstract class Shape {
public abstract void acceptInput(Scanner scanner);
protected abstract double calculateArea();
public void displayArea() {
System.out.printf("Area: %.2f%n", calculateArea());
}
}
class Square extends Shape {
private double side;
@Override
public void acceptInput(Scanner scanner) {
System.out.print("Square side: ");
side = scanner.nextDouble();
}
@Override
protected double calculateArea() {
return side * side;
}
}
class Rectangle extends Shape {
private double length;
private double breadth;
@Override
public void acceptInput(Scanner scanner) {
System.out.print("Rectangle length: ");
length = scanner.nextDouble();
System.out.print("Rectangle breadth: ");
breadth = scanner.nextDouble();
}
@Override
protected double calculateArea() {
return length * breadth;
}
}
class Circle extends Shape {
private double radius;
private static final double PI = 3.14;
@Override
public void acceptInput(Scanner scanner) {
System.out.print("Circle radius: ");
radius = scanner.nextDouble();
}
@Override
protected double calculateArea() {
return PI * radius * radius;
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
```

